Clean Architecture

In fact, many successful applications use Clean Architecture without DDD.

Clean Architecture solves this:
Organizes code into layers
Focuses on dependency direction
Solves architectural concerns
Works for simple and complex apps

1. Domain

This is the heart of the application.

It contains business rules and should have no dependency on Laravel.

Domain/
├── Entities/
├── ValueObjects/
├── Aggregates/
├── Repositories/
├── Events/
└── Services/

class Order
{
    public function confirm()
    {
        if (!$this->paymentReceived()) {
            throw new PaymentRequired();
        }

        $this->status = OrderStatus::Confirmed;
    }
}


2. Application

This layer coordinates use cases.

It answers:

What should happen when a user wants to confirm an order?

Application/
├── Commands/
├── Queries/
├── DTOs/
├── UseCases/
└── Handlers/

class ConfirmOrderHandler
{
    public function handle(ConfirmOrderCommand $command)
    {
        $order = $this->repository->find($command->orderId);

        $order->confirm();

        $this->repository->save($order);
    }
}


Notice:

no HTTP
no database queries
just coordinating the use case


3. Infrastructure

Infrastructure contains technical details.

Infrastructure/
├── Persistence/
├── Queue/
├── Mail/
├── Cache/
├── PaymentGateway/
└── Repositories/

Example:

class EloquentOrderRepository implements OrderRepository
{
    public function find($id)
    {
        return OrderModel::findOrFail($id);
    }
}

Laravel-specific code belongs here.

4. Presentation

Presentation is how users interact with the system.

Examples:

Presentation/
├── Http/
│   ├── Controllers/
│   ├── Requests/
│   └── Resources/
├── Console/
└── GraphQL/

Example:

class OrderController
{
    public function confirm(
        ConfirmOrderRequest $request,
        ConfirmOrderHandler $handler
    ) {
        $handler->handle(
            new ConfirmOrderCommand($request->orderId)
        );

        return response()->json();
    }
}

The controller is intentionally thin.

Request Flow
HTTP Request
      │
      ▼
Presentation
      │
      ▼
Application
      │
      ▼
Domain
      │
      ▼
Infrastructure
      │
      ▼
Database

The important rule is that dependencies point inward:

Presentation depends on Application.
Application depends on Domain.
Infrastructure implements interfaces defined by the Domain or Application.
The Domain depends on nothing else